concept("clickstream", "da", "Clickstream and event tracking", EC, "I",
  "clickstream event tracking tracking plan page view add to cart events properties sessions sessionisation user id anonymous id segment ga4 snowplow",
  "Clickstream data records what users do in the app or website as a stream of events, and a good tracking plan decides whether it can be trusted.",
  "Each event has a name (product_viewed, added_to_cart), a timestamp, a user or anonymous id, a session id, and properties such as product id, price and screen. A tracking plan documents every event, its properties and when it fires, so teams don't invent inconsistent names. Sessionisation groups events into visits, usually ending after 30 minutes of inactivity. Identity stitching links anonymous browsing to the logged-in user. Watch for duplicate events, bots, client clock errors and ad blockers, and validate tracking in QA before releases.",
  "After a release, added_to_cart fired twice per click on Android, doubling cart rates overnight. A tracking-plan check in QA would have caught it before launch.",
  "Why do you need an anonymous id as well as a user id?",
  "Expect to design events for a feature, explain sessionisation, and discuss how tracking errors affect metrics.",
  ["funnels", "event-time", "data-quality"]),
concept("attribution", "da", "Marketing attribution", EC, "A",
  "marketing attribution last click first click linear time decay data driven multi touch channels utm incrementality marketing mix modelling",
  "Attribution assigns credit for a conversion to the marketing touchpoints a customer saw, which drives where the marketing budget goes.",
  "Rule-based models include last click (all credit to the final touch, the most common default), first click, linear (equal shares) and time decay (more credit to recent touches). Data-driven models estimate each channel's contribution statistically. All of them measure correlation, not causation: retargeting often looks great under last click because it targets people about to buy anyway. Incrementality tests, such as geo or holdout experiments, measure the real lift, and marketing mix modelling estimates channel effects from aggregate spend and sales over time. UTM parameters and click ids make tracking possible.",
  "Under last click, branded search gets 40% of credit. A holdout test pausing branded ads in some cities shows most of those buyers arrive anyway through organic search, so its incremental effect is small.",
  "Why does retargeting often look better under last-click attribution than it really is?",
  "Senior marketing analytics interviews ask you to compare attribution models and explain how you'd measure true incrementality.",
  ["ab-testing", "quasi-experiments", "unit-economics"]),
concept("pricing-promotions", "da", "Pricing and promotions analytics", EC, "I",
  "pricing promotions discounts price elasticity coupons promo effectiveness baseline incrementality cannibalisation pull forward margin markdown",
  "Pricing and promotions analytics measures how price changes and discounts affect demand, revenue and margin, beyond the headline sales lift.",
  "Price elasticity is the percentage change in quantity for a 1% change in price; elastic products respond strongly. Promotion analysis compares sales with a baseline of what would have happened without it, from a control group or forecast, to get incremental units. Then subtract the costs: discount given away on sales that would have happened anyway, cannibalisation of other products, pull-forward of future purchases, and the effect on margin. Check whether promotions bring new customers who stay, or just deal-seekers. Markdown analysis decides when to discount ageing stock.",
  "A 20% off coupon lifted shampoo sales 50%, but half the lift came from customers who'd have bought anyway and a rival brand's sales fell 15%, so the net margin effect was negative.",
  "Why isn't the sales uplift during a promotion the same as its impact?",
  "Expect to evaluate a promotion with numbers, explain elasticity, and design a test for a price change.",
  ["unit-economics", "ab-testing", "forecasting"]),
concept("returns-analytics", "da", "Returns analytics", EC, "I",
  "returns analytics return rate rto return to origin refunds reverse logistics reasons size fit damaged cod fraud apparel",
  "Returns cost e-commerce companies heavily in refunds, reverse logistics and damaged stock. Returns analytics finds which products, customers and reasons drive them.",
  "Define return rate carefully: by units or orders, by delivered or shipped, and over what window, since returns arrive weeks later. In India, RTO (return to origin, when a delivery is refused or fails, common with cash on delivery) is tracked separately from customer returns. Break returns down by reason code, category, brand, size, seller, pin code and payment method. Apparel returns are often size and fit problems, fixable with better size charts. Watch for serial returners and fraud, and calculate the full cost of each return, including two-way shipping.",
  "Kurtas from one brand have a 34% return rate, mostly \"too small\". Comparing with its size chart shows it runs a size smaller than others, so the listing adds a \"size up\" note and returns drop to 21%.",
  "Why should return rate be measured on delivered orders over a fixed window?",
  "Expect to define return metrics, find drivers in a dataset, and estimate the cost of returns.",
  ["ecom-data-model", "kpi-metrics", "metric-rca"]),
concept("seller-analytics", "da", "Marketplace seller analytics", EC, "I",
  "marketplace seller analytics seller performance cancellation rate fulfilment sla ratings gmv take rate commission onboarding seller churn",
  "Marketplaces depend on sellers. Seller analytics tracks their performance, health and contribution so the platform can grow good sellers and fix or remove poor ones.",
  "Key seller metrics: GMV and order volume, cancellation rate, shipping on time within the dispatch SLA, return rate, customer ratings and complaints, stock availability, and the platform's take rate (commission and fees as a share of GMV). Seller scorecards combine these, and tiers decide visibility in search and payout terms. Track seller onboarding (time to first sale) and seller churn. Compare sellers within the same category, since return and cancellation norms differ widely.",
  "A seller scorecard flags 120 electronics sellers with cancellation rates above 5%. Lowering their search visibility until they improve cuts customer cancellations by a third.",
  "Why should seller cancellation rates be compared within categories?",
  "Expect to define seller health metrics, build a scorecard, and analyse marketplace supply.",
  ["kpi-metrics", "returns-analytics", "dashboards"]),
concept("personalisation", "da", "Personalisation and product recommendations", EC, "A",
  "personalisation recommendations collaborative filtering content based frequently bought together cold start ranking ctr conversion a/b test",
  "Personalisation shows each customer the products, offers and content most relevant to them, using their behaviour and similar customers' behaviour.",
  "Simple approaches go a long way: best sellers by category and city, \"frequently bought together\" from co-purchase counts, and recently viewed items. Collaborative filtering recommends what similar users liked, while content-based methods use product attributes such as category, brand and price. The cold-start problem affects new users and new products, so fall back to popular or attribute-based items. Measure with online A/B tests on click-through, conversion and revenue per session, not just offline accuracy, and include diversity and business rules such as stock availability.",
  "A \"customers also bought\" widget built from co-purchase counts raised add-to-cart from the product page by 6% in an A/B test, while a complex model tested offline gave only 1% more.",
  "Why must recommendations be judged with an online A/B test rather than only offline accuracy?",
  "Expect approaches to recommendations, the cold-start problem, and how you'd measure success.",
  ["ab-testing", "segmentation", "feature-engineering"]),
concept("availability", "da", "Inventory availability and stock-outs", EC, "I",
  "inventory availability stock outs in stock rate out of stock lost sales fill rate dark stores quick commerce replenishment sku availability",
  "Availability measures whether customers can buy what they want when they look for it. Stock-outs lose sales and push customers to competitors.",
  "In-stock rate can be measured as the share of SKUs available, but a demand-weighted version, the share of product views or searches where the item was available, reflects customer experience better. Lost sales estimates what would have sold during stock-outs, using the normal sales rate. Track availability by SKU, store or dark store and time of day, because quick commerce stores can run out of milk by noon. Root causes include poor forecasts, late replenishment, supplier fill rate and wrong inventory records. Tie availability to the top-selling SKUs, where it matters most.",
  "A dark store's SKU in-stock rate is 96%, but its demand-weighted availability is only 88% because the bestselling bread and milk run out every evening.",
  "Why is demand-weighted availability better than counting in-stock SKUs?",
  "Quick commerce and retail interviews ask how you'd measure availability, estimate lost sales and find root causes of stock-outs.",
  ["inventory-analytics", "forecasting", "kpi-metrics"]),
